The '''Aethopian slave trade''' refers to the forced migration of approximately 12 million enslaved peoples from the [[Kaftia|Kaftian continent]] across the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] to [[Lurando-Abyala]] between 1533 and 1817 as part of the larger [[Kaftian slave trade]]. The trade established the demographic foundations of the contemporary [[Kaftian diaspora]] in the [[Western Hemisphere]] while generating profound cultural transformations in both Kaftia and Lurando-Abyalan colonial society. Operating primarily under Mesogean colonial auspices but involving merchant networks in [[Sanyan]] and other [[Pre-colonial Kaftia|pre-colonial Kaftian states]], the trade differed from the [[The Secret Trade|Shendan slave trade]] in its duration, mortality rates, and the specific ethnic compositions of its captives. The legacy of the Kaftian trade remains central to contemporary Lurando-Abyalan discussions of reparations, cultural heritage, and the socioeconomic status of historically enslaved communities.

==Origins and source regions==
{{Main|Slavery in Kaftia|Owuro-Imfezzan wars}}
[[Sudanese Kaftia]] (roughly corresponding to the all of Kaftia north of the [[Great Kaftian Range]] provided the vast majority of captives for the Aethiopian trade, concentrated in two principal geographic and cultural zones that shaped distinctive patterns of diasporic identity: the interior of what is now [[Ayorin]], encompassing the forest city-states and grassland confederacies along the [[Central Kaftian Plain]] and the [[Tanaruf n-Azref]]. Ayorin at the time was not a unified polity but rather a congeries of competing states—including the x, the [[x Confederacy]], the [[Kingdom of x]], and numerous smaller entities—that engaged in both the capture and sale of enslaved peoples and themselves suffered predation from slaving expeditions. The enslaved peoples brought to Lurando-Abyala spoke diverse languages belonging to the X and X families, practiced complex indigenous religions centered on [[W:Vodún|vodún]] spiritual traditions, and possessed sophisticated metallurgical, agricultural, and political traditions that would profoundly shape subsequent Kafto-Algarvean culture.
[[File:Owuro-Imfezzan wars.jpg|left|thumb|A call to arms ceremony of the [[Owuro Empire|Owuro]], before heading into battle during the [[Owuro-Imfezzan wars]]]]
The [[Tanaruf n-Azref]] (today occupied by the [[Tamazgha|Taghlan of Tamazgha]]) provided the second major source population, though its relationship to the trade was structurally complex. As a vast transcontinental Imfezzan-speaking polity stretching from the [[Bight of Ayorin]] to the [[Kendryian Ocean]] in the east, Tamazgha served simultaneously as a slaving power as part of it's [[Owuro-Imfezzan wars|wars with the Owuro]] (from it's raiding southward into Sudanese Kaftia) and as a source of captives from its own peripheral populations that were captured and sold by the Owuro (particularly as skilled captives including scribes, artisans, and religious Muslims). The Imfezzan war captives transported to Balisca were drawn primarily from the [[Black Imfezzans|Black Imfezzan]] populations of the southern provinces, and defeated factions from inter-Imfezzan warfare.

The visible phenotypic diversity of Kaftian captives created what colonial administrators termed the [[Kaftian color gradient]], a spectrum of complexions ranging from the deep brown and black skin tones of equatorial Ayorin to the reddish-brown and olive complexions of [[North Kaftia|North Kaftians]] from latitudes approaching the [[Arktic Ocean|Arktic (Northern) Ocean]]. Colonial hierarchical ideology struggled to categorize this diversity, eventually producing administrative distinctions between ''Negros de Ayorin'' referring to Ayori captives and moros de Berbería referring to Tamazghi captives. These categories carried material consequences, as lighter-skinned North Kaftians were frequently channeled into domestic service and artisanal trades rather than plantation field labor, and colonial taxation and manumission schedules from the 1680s onward explicitly differentiated between the two groups in legal status and economic value.

